ting their work done well as  people try to make that decision  caroline don't people who are back in  the office  have the upper hand because i think you  need face-to-face interaction with the  powers that be  in order to advance you know yeah i  think that's definitely a concern and  we're seeing a lot of people at linkedin  right now hesitant to return to the  office you know they have some health  concerns  they're nervous to be interacting with  people in person at this point in the  pandemic  but we are seeing companies really step  and up and think more equitably around  how they're treating workers whether  they're at home or they're in the office  and this comes down to individual  managers you just need to make sure that  there isn't that home court advantage if  you will if you are going back into the  office  but americans are also quitting their  jobs in record numbers what's happening  we're calling it the great resignation  we're seeing right now 40 percent of  employers upwards of 40 percent of  people are thinking about quitting their  jobs right now and  you know anthony why i think the  pandemic as tony just said  made us re-examine everything about our  lives and if you think about  pre-pandemic  jobs was a big part of everyone's life  and now people are saying i don't know  how big i wanted to be a part of my life  now but one theory is that pre-pandemic  you liked the office you liked lunch you  liked your friends at work you liked  your commute you liked your quiet time  now you're left with none of that and  just the work itself and you're  realizing  i don't like this work this is not fun  exactly if you think about like the  perks of going back into the office  before the pandemic started for  knowledge workers it was all about the  free lunch or the ping pong tables or  being able to go to happy hours  i think we're still the point right now  where those perks aren't back and so yes  a lot of people are saying  can i continue to work from home and the  reality is you know  there's a huge talent shortage right now  so if you are a valuable employee 70 of  companies are telling us they're having  a hard time attracting talent finding  the right talent so employees have upper  hand what are companies doing what are  companies doing to respond to this well  they have to do a lot they have to do it  very quickly because 70 of companies are  saying that they're having a hard time  finding talent and you have all these  people trying to quit work and so  they're responding by  a lot of things number one is providing  this flexibility if you're  at a place that doesn't have flexible  options there are a lot of places that  are offering it  the other is mental health i think that  mental health is now considered the  fourth  pillar of benefits right very  interesting and so it was something that  wasn't offered before but now after  we've seen the pandemic  after everyone's been working from home  and seeing their whole lives employers  can't ignore the fact that their  employees are not robots  and they have a bunch of stuff going on  outside of work this is particularly  important for working parents  and doesn't give employees a better an  upper hand during these times  absolutely i think if you performed well  during the pandemic you continue to  perform well you continue to have this  upper hand because like i said so many  employers are having a hard time  finding talent and you still love your  job i still love my job tony so i'm not  being pretty  unless gayle wants to hire me i work for  gayle but other than that i'm not  quitting anything welcome back to  in-person work i want to hang out with  you carol  i still like my job too thank you good  to see you
 

